# frozen_string_literal: true

# Generated by shipshape. This file is yours now.
#
# **A write to something outside this process** — a payment taken, an invoice sent, a
# webhook delivered. The `Io` prefix on the class name means the crossing is visible at
# every call site without opening anything.
#
# It is a **sister of Command**, not a subclass of one: every class inherits exactly one
# base and no deeper, so the contract is repeated here rather than inherited.
#
# **Why it is a separate kind at all, and the reason is the transaction.** A command is
# exactly one transaction. An external call made inside one holds a database transaction
# open across a network round trip — the classic way a slow third party takes a database
# down — and the remote write cannot be rolled back by it in any case. So a command may not
# do IO. The external call and the local write that records its result are two steps,
# sequenced by a workflow, which is the only kind that has accepted the bill for spanning
# them: each step idempotent, each intermediate state legal.
#
# **It opens no transaction and touches no record**, for the same reason. What it changes is
# somebody else's, and what it answers with is a Result the caller can act on.
#
# **Assume it will be retried.** A workflow's only recovery is to run the sequence again, so
# send an idempotency key where the far end offers one, and where it does not, say so in the
# subclass rather than hoping.
class IoCommand
  include TypedArguments

<%- if auth -%>
  extend Permission
<%- end -%>

  # The check runs here, once, for every command — not in each `call`, where it would be a
  # line every operation has to remember and one operation would forget. It opens no transaction of its own.
  #
  # An operation implementing `anonymous_call` instead of `call` runs before anyone is
  # identified and is not checked. That is a property of the class, never of the caller.
<%- if auth -%>
  def self.call(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless permits?(actor)
      # **The refusal is the entry nobody has when they need it.** Recorded before returning,
      # because a caller who was told no is exactly what somebody comes looking for.
      AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: :refused, actor: actor, error: :forbidden,
                      arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)
      return Result.failure(:forbidden)
    end

    operation = new(**arguments)
    result = operation.__perform__(actor)
<%- else -%>
  def self.call(**arguments)
    result = new(**arguments).__perform__
<%- end -%>

<%- if auth -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the transaction, never inside it.** An entry written inside is erased by the
    # rollback it exists to explain.
    AuditLog.record(
      operation: name,
      outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
      actor: actor,
      error: result.error,
      arguments: arguments,
      excluded: excluded_from_audit,
    )

    result
  end
<%- else -%>
    raise TypeError, "#{name}#call must answer with a Result, got #{result.class}" unless result.is_a?(Result)

    # **After the transaction, never inside it.** An entry written inside is erased by the
    # rollback it exists to explain.
    AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: result.success? ? :succeeded : :failed,
                    error: result.error, arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)

    result
  end
<%- end -%>

  private
<%- if auth -%>

  # Whoever the door was opened for. Most operations never read it, which is why it is here
  # rather than in every initializer.
  attr_reader :actor

<%- end -%>

  def success(value = nil)
    Result.success(value)
  end

  def failure(error)
    Result.failure(error)
  end

  # **Dispatches with an implicit receiver**, so it works whether the operation left its
  # entry point public or made it private. `send` would work too and is banned: it steps
  # over `private`, routes around the call graph, and names a method nobody can grep for. A
  # base class that writes it cannot ask an application not to.
  #
  # Public, and unreachable all the same: `new` is private, so nobody outside can hold an
  # operation to call this on — and `Shipshape/OnlyTheDoorIsCalled` refuses the attempt at
  # the call site, where no visibility keyword can be stepped over.
<%- if auth -%>
  public def __perform__(actor)
    # **The actor belongs to the base class, not to the operation's signature.** The check
    # above needed it; most operations do not, and forcing `actor:` into every initializer
    # would put a keyword in fifty constructors so that three could read it. Set here, and
    # available to any operation that wants it through the private reader below.
    @actor = actor

    self.class.anonymous? ? anonymous_call : call
  end
<%- else -%>
  public def __perform__
    call
  end
<%- end -%>

<%- if auth -%>
  # **Deferred, at the one grain where deferral is safe:** one command, one transaction, one
  # job, so a retry re-runs exactly one transaction. `a-command-runs-twice` is what makes that
  # retry harmless, and it is a law rather than a hope.
  #
  # **The Result describes the enqueue, never the work.** `success(:enqueued)` means it was
  # accepted, not that it happened — there is no caller to tell what happened, which is why
  # every door reports to the audit log. A deferred failure is recorded there exactly as a
  # synchronous one is.
  #
  # Permission is checked **twice, on purpose**: here so the caller learns immediately that it
  # was refused, and again when the job runs, because a right revoked in between is the answer
  # that matters. The refusal here is audited like any other.
  def self.call_later(actor: nil, **arguments)
    unless permits?(actor)
      AuditLog.record(operation: name, outcome: :refused, actor: actor, error: :forbidden,
                      arguments: arguments, excluded: excluded_from_audit)
      return Result.failure(:forbidden)
    end

    # **Built and thrown away, so the arguments are asserted here.** Without this
    # `call_later` accepted what `call` refuses — a wrong type, or a record — and answered
    # `success(:enqueued)` for work that could never run, which then burned its whole retry
    # budget failing. An initializer only asserts, so building one twice costs nothing.
    new(**arguments)
    refuse_unidentifiable(actor)

    OperationJob.set(queue: queue_name).perform_later(
      operation: name,
      arguments: arguments,
      actor_class: actor&.class&.name,
      actor_id: actor.respond_to?(:id) ? actor.id : nil,
    )

    Result.success(:enqueued)
  end

  # **An actor that cannot be named cannot be deferred.** Nothing here says an actor is a
  # record — `permits?` needs only `may?` — so an actor with no `id` is legitimate and used to
  # be dropped silently: the caller was told `success(:enqueued)`, the job then died
  # "requires an actor", exhausted its retries and wrote no audit entry at all. Refusing at
  # the door is the same failure the argument assertion above exists to prevent, one keyword
  # over.
  def self.refuse_unidentifiable(actor)
    return if actor.nil?
    return if actor.respond_to?(:id) && !actor.id.nil?

    raise ArgumentError, "#{name}.call_later needs an actor with an id to defer; " \
                         "#{actor.class} has none, so the job could not rebuild it"
  end

  private_class_method :refuse_unidentifiable

<%- else -%>
  # **Deferred, at the one grain where deferral is safe:** one command, one transaction, one
  # job, so a retry re-runs exactly one transaction. `a-command-runs-twice` is what makes that
  # retry harmless, and it is a law rather than a hope.
  #
  # **The Result describes the enqueue, never the work.** `success(:enqueued)` means it was
  # accepted, not that it happened — there is no caller to tell what happened, which is why
  # every door reports to the audit log.
  def self.call_later(**arguments)
    new(**arguments)

    OperationJob.set(queue: queue_name).perform_later(operation: name, arguments: arguments)

    Result.success(:enqueued)
  end
<%- end -%>
  # **Which queue, declared on the operation.** `QUEUE = :payments` on the class; the default
  # otherwise. `ATTEMPTS = 2` is declared the same way and read by OperationJob at the moment
  # it decides whether to retry — not with `retry_on`, which would fix one policy for every
  # operation because it captures its limit when the job class is defined.
  def self.queue_name
    const_defined?(:QUEUE, false) ? const_get(:QUEUE, false) : :default
  end

  private_class_method :queue_name

  # **The only way in is `call`.** A caller cannot build an operation, so it cannot reach one
  # that skipped the permission check, the transaction or the return-type assertion.
  #
  # `allocate` is on the list because it is the hole `new` alone leaves: it is public on every
  # Ruby class, it skips `initialize` entirely, and `SettleInvoice.allocate.__perform__(actor)`
  # ran the operation unauthenticated. Verified by running it, not by reading it.
  # **What this operation keeps out of the trail**, beyond what `PersonalData` already
  # declares. For a value that is not a column and so is in no registry: a token, a free-text
  # note, a payload.
  def self.excluded_from_audit
    const_defined?(:EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT, false) ? const_get(:EXCLUDE_FROM_AUDIT, false) : []
  end

  private_class_method :excluded_from_audit

  private_class_method :new, :allocate
end
